          },
          {
            "alt": "A photograph shows the head and part of the body of a lizard on a rock in a well-lit area.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "An agama lizard basks in the sun. As its body warms, the chemical reactions of its metabolism speed up."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "g7rzjwbwg4js8zz",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "26fyx89mpl2mwgb",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/71/71439890223abc0f474741b8ef6cdb13/CNX_Chem_12_00_Ectotherm.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "59cr1n5tr4uety5",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The lizard in the photograph is not simply enjoying the sunshine or working on its tan. The heat from the sun’s rays is critical to the lizard’s survival. A warm lizard can move faster than a cold one because the chemical reactions that allow its muscles to move occur more rapidly at higher temperatures. In the absence of warmth, the lizard is an easy meal for predators."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3758081775",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "From baking a cake to determining the useful lifespan of a bridge, rates of chemical reactions are important to our understanding of processes that involve chemical changes. When planning to run a chemical reaction, we should ask at least two questions: "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "Will the reaction produce the desired products in useful quantities?"
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "How rapidly will the reaction occur?"
              },
              {
                "text": " A reaction that takes 50 years to produce a product is about as useful as one that never gives a product at all. A third question is often asked when investigating reactions in greater detail: "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "What specific molecular-level processes take place as the reaction occurs?"
              },
              {
                "text": " Knowing the answer to this question is of practical importance when the yield or rate of a reaction needs to be controlled."
              }
            ],
            "id": "t5y6mu00zaivgdh",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The study of chemical kinetics concerns the second and third questions—that is, the rate at which a reaction yields a product and the molecular-scale means by which a reaction occurs. In this unit, we examine the factors that influence the rates of chemical reactions, the mechanisms by which reactions proceed, and the quantitative techniques used to determine and describe the rate at which reactions occur."
              }
            ],
            "id": "nwa784pt83bi8cr",
            "type": "p"
          },
